g  that the  the range of symptoms should be expanded  and this has been clear from  the people reporting on our app where  there are over 20 symptoms of covid that  are linked  and we've done uh we've published papers  and continues to do research saying the  same thing's happening is that 30  percent of people  who have a positive pcr test uh as  perhaps you did  do not have those three classic symptoms  and  this is particularly true in the first  three days of the illness  when it's most infectious so  i understand why we didn't do this last  spring because we didn't have any  testing  and it would have crashed the system  now we have a fantastic testing system  able to do over 600 000 tests a day  we do have the capacity to do it this is  the time to do that and we would reduce  infections by 30 in reality because  these people would be told to  stay at home at the moment it's you know  it's a real risk that we are ignoring  and all these smoke screens of testing  asymptomatic people  is far less productive than getting to  these people that  actually have symptoms and are spreading  the virus around so absolutely  um and i've forgotten your other  question  that was it i think that's all good oh i  did ask if the new variant  uh leads to different symptoms it's  interesting that  the the jury's out on this the the ons  uh  survey which is a similar size to us but  very different methodology  showed that there was more evidence of  headaches and fatigue  and um sort of sore throats  uh whereas our data which has been  consistent since since the beginning  has shown no difference so uh i i think  we don't know yet  i think we need to keep a close eye on  it but if everyone was more aware  that you know the two common symptoms of  early covid are headache and fatigue and  sort  and then you've got sore throat and sore  muscles then  actually everyone would be safer so i  think it's it's a good debate to keep  going on and keep looking at  now you said recently that you don't  watch the number 10 news conferences  anymore  why not um  well i i i've overdone on covert stats i  guess  and i also um i i also  get a bit disheartened by the fact that  we only focus on certain of those  statistics so  the fact that not just the number 10 but  also the media  focus on the number of deaths that day  and i think it's a very misleading  statistic  because um i can't know whether  it's very sad people die of anything  but for example yesterday around 600  people died of covid  but uh on a normal  uh day in february 1500 people would die  mostly of heart disease strokes um  cancer etc so or the flu  so we never put those statistics into  any  uh context and i think this has got a  lot of people  extremely anxious um and  you know are petrified to leave their  homes and may have real problems  coming out again if we don't put these  stats  into some real perspective that yes  rates are high people are dying from  covid but they also die from other  things  many of those people are not seeking  help in the nhs  they are having mental health problems  uh they're not getting their cancer  looked at  not looking after their heart etc so i  just would like to see  also uh perhaps less  fear-mongering and more a general  picture and that's  oh we've just lost you um we did get uh  through most of the interview though uh  fascinating stuff professor  tim spector there uh from kings the  founder of the zoe app

